    "sql-order-limit-learn": {
      "id": "sql-order-limit-learn",
      "title": "Learn: the order is a promise you make",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "sql-order-limit"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain what ORDER BY promises about the rows, and what it leaves open.",
        "Predict the order of the rows when two of them hold the same value.",
        "Write a query that returns the first few rows of a report in the order the report asks for."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Run the example in examples.sql and look at the dates. Two of them are the same, which is the case the lesson is about.",
        "The order is plain SQL, so the rows do not depend on the tool: Node 22.5 and newer carries SQLite as node:sqlite, and the sqlite3 command line is the other way in."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking the rows come back in the order they were written once an ORDER BY names one column.",
        "Thinking LIMIT drops rows by value, when it keeps the first rows of the order and stops."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "SQLite: the SELECT statement, the ORDER BY and LIMIT clauses",
          "url": "https://sqlite.org/lang_select.html"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The order of the rows is not promised until you promise it. ORDER BY names the column to sort by, and DESC turns the sort around, so the largest balance comes first. LIMIT then keeps the first rows of that order and drops the rest."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A promise of an order has to cover the case where two rows are equal. ORDER BY owner ASC, id ASC says the names come in alphabetical order and, where two accounts have the same name, the earlier id comes first. Without the second column, the order between two equal rows is not promised."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The transactions of one account, the newest first, as examples.sql writes it:",
          "code": "SELECT id, posted_at\nFROM transactions\nWHERE account_id = 'a-003'\nORDER BY posted_at DESC;"
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The same idea the other way round, and with a rule for rows that tie:",
          "code": "SELECT owner, cents\nFROM accounts\nORDER BY cents ASC;"
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "ORDER BY promises the order.",
            "DESC puts the largest first.",
            "LIMIT keeps the first rows.",
            "A tie needs a second column."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the checks for this phase:",
          "code": "node --test --test-reporter=tap test/order-limit.test.mjs"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open queries/03-order-limit.sql and write the query the file asks for: the three accounts with the largest balance, largest first."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "query-plan",
        "spec": {
          "steps": [
            {
              "label": "the accounts table",
              "rows": 6
            },
            {
              "label": "ORDER BY cents DESC",
              "rows": 6
            },
            {
              "label": "LIMIT 3",
              "rows": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "caption": "ORDER BY promises an order and changes no rows. LIMIT then keeps the first three of that order and drops the rest."
      }
    },
