    "sql-group-by-learn": {
      "id": "sql-group-by-learn",
      "title": "Learn: one number for many rows",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "sql-group-by"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain what an aggregate function computes over a set of rows.",
        "Predict what sum() gives for a group with no rows, and why.",
        "Write a query that counts the rows of each group and keeps only the groups that matter."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Every account in this ledger has a balance, and the sum of its transactions is the same number. Phase 4 asks you to prove it.",
        "The totals are plain SQL, so the numbers do not depend on the tool: Node 22.5 and newer carries SQLite as node:sqlite, and the sqlite3 command line is the other way in."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking count(*) counts the values in a column, when it counts the rows of the group.",
        "Thinking the sum of a group with no rows is zero, when the standard says it is nothing at all."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "SQLite: built-in aggregate functions",
          "url": "https://sqlite.org/lang_aggfunc.html"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "An aggregate function reads many rows and gives back one number. count() counts, sum() adds up, min() and max() take the ends, and avg() divides. A query that mixes a plain column with an aggregate is asking for one number per group, so the rows have to be gathered into groups first."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "GROUP BY says which column makes the groups. HAVING then keeps the groups, not the rows, which is the difference between the two clauses. It is the clause to reach for when the condition is about a number the aggregate computed."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The two ends of the ledger in one row, as examples.sql writes it:",
          "code": "SELECT max(cents) AS biggest, min(cents) AS smallest\nFROM transactions;"
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "An average per account, and the groups in account id order:",
          "code": "SELECT account_id, avg(cents) AS average_amount\nFROM transactions\nGROUP BY account_id\nORDER BY account_id;"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "One rule is worth knowing before it surprises you. sum() over no rows gives nothing at all, not zero, because the SQL standard says so. avg() always gives a fraction, even when every amount is a whole number."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "An aggregate gives one number.",
            "GROUP BY gathers the groups.",
            "HAVING keeps the groups.",
            "The sum of no rows is nothing."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the checks for this phase:",
          "code": "node --test --test-reporter=tap test/group-by.test.mjs"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open queries/04-group-by.sql and write the query the file asks for: the accounts with at least three transactions, with the count, in account id order."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "query-plan",
        "spec": {
          "steps": [
            {
              "label": "the transactions table",
              "rows": 12
            },
            {
              "label": "GROUP BY account_id",
              "rows": 4
            },
            {
              "label": "count(*) for each group",
              "rows": 4
            }
          ]
        },
        "caption": "Twelve rows go in and four rows come out. The group is the unit, and the aggregate gives one number for each group."
      }
    },
