    "sql-joins-learn": {
      "id": "sql-joins-learn",
      "title": "Learn: two tables read as one",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "sql-joins"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain what a join matches, and what the ON clause names.",
        "Predict which rows a join keeps when one side has no match.",
        "Write a query that returns a row from each of the two tables of the ledger."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Look at the two tables in schema.sql before you write the join. One holds the accounts, the other holds the transactions that belong to them.",
        "The join is plain SQL, so the matched rows do not depend on the tool: Node 22.5 and newer carries SQLite as node:sqlite, and the sqlite3 command line is the other way in."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking the join adds the columns of the second table to the rows of the first one.",
        "Thinking a foreign key is checked by SQLite whether the application asks for it or not."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "SQLite: the JOIN clause",
          "url": "https://sqlite.org/lang_join.html"
        },
        {
          "label": "SQLite: foreign key support",
          "url": "https://sqlite.org/foreignkeys.html"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The transactions table does not hold the name of the owner. It holds the id of the account the transaction belongs to, and the name is in the accounts table. A join is how the two tables are read together: it matches the rows of one table against the rows of the other, and the ON clause says which column decides the match."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The inner join keeps the pairs that match. A row of one table with no partner in the other is left out. That is what a transaction on an account that is not in the accounts table would meet, and the ledger has none, because the column names the account."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The transactions of one account, with the name of the owner, as examples.sql writes it:",
          "code": "SELECT t.id, a.owner, t.cents\nFROM transactions t\nJOIN accounts a ON a.id = t.account_id\nWHERE a.owner = 'Gita'\nORDER BY t.id;"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The letters t and a are names for the two tables in the query. They keep the columns apart when both tables have a column of the same name, and they make the query shorter to read."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "A join matches rows.",
            "ON names the match.",
            "Only matched pairs stay.",
            "The alias names a table."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the checks for this phase:",
          "code": "node --test --test-reporter=tap test/joins.test.mjs"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open queries/05-joins.sql and write the query the file asks for: the transactions on the accounts that owe money, with the owner and the amount, in transaction id order."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "state-compare",
        "spec": {
          "before": {
            "title": "transactions alone",
            "lines": [
              "t-001 | 199",
              "t-002 | -50"
            ]
          },
          "after": {
            "title": "joined with accounts",
            "lines": [
              "t-001 | Ada | 199",
              "t-002 | Gita | -50"
            ]
          }
        },
        "caption": "The join adds the owner from the other table and adds no rows. A transaction belongs to one account, so each one gains a name and keeps its own row."
      }
    },
