    "sql-indexes-learn": {
      "id": "sql-indexes-learn",
      "title": "Learn: another table, sorted",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "sql-indexes"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain what an index holds, and how the planner uses it.",
        "Read a query plan and say whether the engine scans the table or searches it.",
        "Write the statement that adds an index, and the query that shows the plan."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Ask for the plan yourself: EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN goes in front of the query, and the answer is a list of steps.",
        "The plan is printed by the engine, so the steps do not depend on the tool: Node 22.5 and newer carries SQLite as node:sqlite, and the sqlite3 command line is the other way in."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking an index is part of the data, when it is another copy of part of it.",
        "Thinking a search in the plan means the index holds the rows, when it holds the keys."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "SQLite: query planning",
          "url": "https://sqlite.org/queryplanner.html"
        },
        {
          "label": "SQLite: the EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN command",
          "url": "https://sqlite.org/eqp.html"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Without an index, SQLite answers a lookup by reading every row of the table and checking it. With an index, it reads the index, which is sorted, finds the row it needs and goes straight to it. The index is another table in the same file, so it takes space and every write to the table has to write to it too."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN prints the steps the engine will take. SCAN means every row is read. SEARCH means only some of them are, and the line names the index that found them. A line that says USE TEMP B-TREE FOR ORDER BY means the rows were found quickly and then sorted in memory anyway."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The index and the two plans, as examples.sql writes it:",
          "code": "CREATE INDEX idx_accounts_owner ON accounts(owner);"
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "And the plan of a lookup, before and after the index:",
          "code": "--   SCAN accounts\n--   SEARCH accounts USING INDEX idx_accounts_owner (owner=?)"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "One promise the documentation makes: an index never changes the answer. It changes how the engine gets to the answer and nothing else, so the rows a query returns are the same with and without it."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "An index is a sorted copy.",
            "SCAN reads every row.",
            "SEARCH reads some rows.",
            "The answer never changes."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the checks for this phase:",
          "code": "node --test --test-reporter=tap test/indexes.test.mjs"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open queries/07-indexes.sql and write the statement that adds the index and the query that reads the transactions of one account."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "query-plan",
        "spec": {
          "steps": [
            {
              "label": "SCAN accounts",
              "rows": 6
            },
            {
              "label": "SEARCH with the index",
              "rows": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "caption": "The index never changes the answer. It changes the work: six rows read becomes one row read."
      }
    },
