    "sql-transactions-learn": {
      "id": "sql-transactions-learn",
      "title": "Learn: all of it or none of it",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "sql-transactions"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain what a transaction promises about the writes it holds.",
        "Describe the isolation SQLite gives between two connections, and where it differs from other engines.",
        "Write a transaction that commits one change and throws another away."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Every statement runs in a transaction of its own unless you say otherwise, so the ledger is never half written by accident.",
        "The commit and the rollback are plain SQL, so the outcome does not depend on the tool: Node 22.5 and newer carries SQLite as node:sqlite, and the sqlite3 command line is the other way in."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking a transaction is a way to make a slow query faster.",
        "Thinking a second connection can see the rows of a transaction that has not been committed."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "SQLite: the transaction control syntax",
          "url": "https://sqlite.org/lang_transaction.html"
        },
        {
          "label": "SQLite: isolation in SQLite",
          "url": "https://sqlite.org/isolation.html"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A transaction is a set of writes that all happen or none of them happen. BEGIN opens one, COMMIT keeps the writes, and ROLLBACK throws them away. Without a transaction, every statement is one of its own, so a script that writes three rows and fails on the second leaves the first row behind."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "SQLite runs one writer at a time and lets any number of readers read at the same time. A reader sees only what has been committed: the rows of an open transaction are invisible to it. In the default rollback journal mode the transactions are serializable, and in WAL mode a reader holds a snapshot of the file as it was when the read began."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Two rules are worth knowing. A transaction cannot be started inside another one, so a nested change needs a SAVEPOINT. And a transaction sees its own writes: the same connection reads back the rows it has written but not yet committed."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "A transaction that is thrown away again, as examples.sql writes it:",
          "code": "BEGIN;\nINSERT INTO transactions (id, account_id, cents, memo, posted_at)\nVALUES ('t-950', 'a-001', 50, 'example', '2026-03-16');\nROLLBACK;"
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "And the one that is kept:",
          "code": "BEGIN;\nINSERT INTO transactions (id, account_id, cents, memo, posted_at)\nVALUES ('t-951', 'a-001', 50, 'example', '2026-03-16');\nCOMMIT;"
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "All of it or none of it.",
            "One writer at a time.",
            "A reader sees commits.",
            "A nested change needs a savepoint."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the checks for this phase:",
          "code": "node --test --test-reporter=tap test/transactions.test.mjs"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open queries/08-transactions.sql and write the transaction that inserts the row and commits it, then the select that shows it."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "state-compare",
        "spec": {
          "before": {
            "title": "a reader, while the write is open",
            "lines": [
              "the new row is not there",
              "the count is 41"
            ]
          },
          "after": {
            "title": "the same reader, after COMMIT",
            "lines": [
              "the new row is there",
              "the count is 42"
            ]
          }
        },
        "caption": "A reader sees the rows of a committed transaction and not the rows of one that is still open. The same query, before and after the commit."
      }
    },
