    "sql-schema-learn": {
      "id": "sql-schema-learn",
      "title": "Learn: the schema is text",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "sql-schema"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain why SQLite keeps the schema as text, and what that makes hard to change.",
        "Name the changes ALTER TABLE can make, and the ones it cannot.",
        "Write a change that keeps every row, using the procedure the documentation gives."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "The shape of the ledger is in schema.sql, and the rows are in seed.sql. A change to the shape is a change to the first one.",
        "The new column is plain SQL, so the shape does not depend on the tool: Node 22.5 and newer carries SQLite as node:sqlite, and the sqlite3 command line is the other way in."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking ALTER TABLE can change any column of a table in one statement.",
        "Thinking a change to a table is only a change to the text of its statement, with no rows involved."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "SQLite: the ALTER TABLE statement",
          "url": "https://sqlite.org/lang_altertable.html"
        },
        {
          "label": "SQLite: the sqlite_schema table",
          "url": "https://sqlite.org/schematab.html"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "SQLite keeps the schema as the text of the statements that made the tables, in a table called sqlite_schema. Every time the file is opened, that text is read back and parsed. It is a small file and it stays readable across versions, and it is also why changing a table is harder here than it is in a server engine."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "ALTER TABLE can do four things in SQLite: rename the table, rename a column, add a column and drop a column. Anything else has to be done in the safe way: build a new table with the shape you want, copy the rows into it, drop the old one, rename the new one into its place, and do all of it inside one transaction."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The four changes SQLite can make, as examples.sql writes them:",
          "code": "--   ALTER TABLE accounts RENAME COLUMN cents TO balance_cents;\n--   ALTER TABLE accounts ADD COLUMN opened_at TEXT;\n--   ALTER TABLE accounts DROP COLUMN opened_at;"
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "And the shape of the safe way, in the order the documentation gives it:",
          "code": "CREATE TABLE statements_new (\n  id         TEXT    PRIMARY KEY,\n  account_id TEXT    NOT NULL REFERENCES accounts(id),\n  cents      INTEGER NOT NULL\n);"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The copy is the part that goes wrong. A copy that leaves a row out, or that copies the rows in the wrong order, changes the ledger without saying so, and the file that made the change is the only place the mistake can be seen."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "The schema is text.",
            "Four changes, no more.",
            "Build, copy, drop, rename.",
            "Do it in a transaction."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the checks for this phase:",
          "code": "node --test --test-reporter=tap test/schema.test.mjs"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open queries/09-schema.sql and write the change the file asks for: a currency column on the accounts table, and one account in another currency."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "event-flow",
        "spec": {
          "steps": [
            "build the new table",
            "copy the rows",
            "drop the old table",
            "rename the new one"
          ]
        },
        "caption": "The four steps run inside one transaction, so a failure anywhere leaves the old table exactly as it was."
      }
    },
