    "sql-schema-ask": {
      "id": "sql-schema-ask",
      "title": "Reason: why the safe way is the safe way",
      "kind": "question",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "review",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "sql-schema"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Ask what a transaction gives you when the copy goes wrong halfway."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking the copy is the easy part of the change.",
        "Thinking dropping the old table before copying the rows is the shorter way round."
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A table has to change in a way ALTER TABLE cannot do. The documentation says to build a new table, copy the rows, drop the old one and rename the new one. Why does it say to do all of that inside one transaction?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        "So a copy that goes wrong leaves the old table exactly as it was.",
        "So the copy of the rows runs faster inside a transaction.",
        "So the new table cannot be read while the old one is still there.",
        "So SQLite will not let the table be changed without one."
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explanation": "A transaction is all of it or none of it: a failed copy or an unfinished rename leaves the ledger as it was. The order and the transaction make it safe: a new table is built beside the old, the rows are copied, and only then the old is dropped and the new renamed into place. Outside a transaction, a failure halfway leaves no table at all."
    },
